# Chapter Thirteen — White Paper

= Draft: dramatic conversation cut 0.1. Act Three begins. The correction packet is recovered under lawful authority and Ruth's account moves a half step. The working crime engine remains unratified. No interaction branches yet.

## Scene 39 — Through the bag

= Present: Henry, Nell, Ward, and a property clerk; Lydia is brought in for the reels.

= Immediate pressure: Nell is here to identify her mother's property in a room that treats it as evidence. She will get to see it and not to have it.

INT. POLICE HEADQUARTERS, PROPERTY ROOM - MORNING

Police headquarters keeps its property room in the basement. At twenty past nine Saturday morning, a clerk with a rubber thumb sets two clear bags on a steel counter and stands back from them.

Ward takes the first bag by its sealed corner and turns it toward Nell.

Inside is a blue folder. Its cover reads PRIVATE WEATHER in a hand Nell has seen on a continuity ledger and, before that, on lunch bags.

WARD
Through the bag. Do not open it.

NELL
I know.

She bends over the counter. Her hands stay at her sides.

NELL
Turn it.

Ward turns the bag over. On the back of the folder, in the lower corner, is a name written in blue pencil. The capital J was made backward first and then corrected.

NELL
That is hers.

WARD
The writing?

NELL
The folder. The writing. She wrote her name on things so Marian would stop putting them away.

Ward writes on a form.

WARD
The reels are Miss Shaw's to identify.

The clerk opens the inner door. Lydia has been waiting on the other side of it. She is in her traveling coat, without the suitcase. She comes to the counter and stands beside Nell, not close.

The second bag holds six small reels in production boxes. The leader of each reel carries a number in green ink.

LYDIA
One to six.

WARD
Your hand?

LYDIA
Mine. I wrote them on her kitchen table the week the picture stopped. She was making coffee and said if I was going to number her life I could at least—

She stops. Nobody has stopped her.

NELL
At least what?

LYDIA
I don't remember. I have told that story for fourteen years and I have never once reached the end of it.

Nell looks at the reels, then at Lydia.

NELL
Then that is where it ends.

LYDIA
Yes.

Ward writes.

LYDIA
There is a train Monday.

NELL
Edwin told me.

Lydia nods and does not go. Nell looks at the reels for a while.

NELL
I heard one yesterday.

WARD
Havel's transfer.

NELL
It was her. She coughed.

She looks up.

NELL
May I hold it?

Ward looks at the clerk. The clerk looks at the ceiling.

WARD
Through the bag.

Nell picks up the bag with the folder inside it. She holds it in both hands the way a person holds something that might be hot.

NELL
It weighs nothing.

WARD
Paper.

NELL
She carried this home on the streetcar every night for eleven days. Robert said she came in the door in her coat and would not take it off until she had put this somewhere.

Nobody says anything.

NELL
Somewhere was the hall closet. And then Paul's room. And then a warehouse. And now here.

She puts the bag down exactly where it was.

NELL
When do I get it?

WARD
When it stops being evidence. There will be an ownership finding first. You have made a claim; so has Ellery's estate, by default.

NELL
Ruth.

WARD
Ruth.

Nell signs the identification form without reading it. Henry has not moved from the doorway.

## Scene 40 — The corridor

= Present: Henry, Nell, Ward, and Ruth.

= Immediate pressure: Ruth has just signed the account that fits the whiting. Ward has spent the night deciding what it does not fit.

INT. POLICE HEADQUARTERS, INTERVIEW CORRIDOR - MORNING

The corridor outside the interview rooms has a bench, a water fountain, and a clock that is four minutes fast. Ruth comes out of the second door with a carbon of her statement folded in her hand. She sees Nell first.

RUTH
Did you identify it?

NELL
Yes.

RUTH
Good.

NELL
You keep saying good.

RUTH
I keep meaning it.

Ward comes out behind her. He does not sit.

WARD
Miss Ellery. I am applying for a warrant for your office this morning. I expect to have it by one.

RUTH
You have my file.

WARD
I am not looking for the file.

RUTH
Then what?

WARD
Paper that is not in it.

Ruth folds the carbon once more.

RUTH
When?

WARD
This afternoon. You may be present. You may bring a lawyer. If you do not bring a lawyer you may bring one other person, and that person stands where I put them and touches nothing.

Ruth looks at Henry.

RUTH
Will you come?

HENRY
As what?

RUTH
As the one person there who will not perform for them.

Henry looks at Nell.

HENRY
I work for you.

NELL
I hired you to find my mother's property. Her property is downstairs in a bag.

RUTH
Mine is not.

NELL
No.

Nell picks up her handbag.

NELL
Go. Then tell me badly.

She walks toward the stairs. Ruth watches her go.

RUTH
She keeps her promises.

HENRY
She made that one to me.

RUTH
I know whose it was.

## Scene 41 — White paper

= Present: Henry, Ruth, Ward, and two officers.

= Immediate pressure: Ward has authority to search. Ruth has asked for a witness who will not improve what is found. Henry stood in this room yesterday.

INT. RUTH'S OFFICE - AFTERNOON

At five past two, the presses beneath Ruth's office are silent. On Saturday the printer does not open, and the stair rail does not tremble.

Ward has the warrant in an envelope and two officers with him. One carries a camera. Ruth unlocks the frosted door herself and stands aside.

WARD
Calder. There.

He puts Henry beside the outer door, with his back to the frosted glass. Ruth is put beside the typewriter table, where she can see both rooms.

The officers begin in the inner office. Drawers come out and go back. The desk diary is photographed open at Wednesday. The filing wall gives up nothing that is not advertising copy. Ward reads a few sheets and puts them back where they were.

Ruth watches the inner office. She does not watch the worktable.

Ward comes out and stands beside Henry without looking at him.

WARD
You were in this room yesterday.

HENRY
Yes.

WARD
Did you see the worktable?

HENRY
I saw white paper over four photographs.

WARD
Did you lift it?

HENRY
No.

WARD
Why not?

HENRY
She said what was open on her desk was not mine.

WARD
That is a worktable.

HENRY
Yes.

Ward goes back into the inner office.

The second officer comes out and starts on the outer room. The typewriter table has one drawer of ribbons and carbon. The long worktable holds the four product photographs Henry looked at on Friday: the floor wax, the unused kitchen, the smiling man, the blank rectangle where the price should be. White paper lies over the words beneath each of them.

The officer lifts the first overlay by its corner.

The underside is typed.

He turns it over on the table. It is a full sheet, bond, whole. The heading reads:

!CORRECTED DECLARATION — 418-R — P. ELLERY

WARD
Photograph it where it lies.

The camera goes off. The second overlay is typed too. The third is not the declaration. It is a copy of a scene page from the blue folder, attached so that a production office would know which scene was meant. Its upper corner is gone, and the heading with it. Henry knows what the heading was. He read it in a dead man's hand. The fourth, over the smiling man, is a half sheet. The officer turns it.

At the bottom, typed:

PRIVATE RECORDING?

Beneath it, in pencil:

!REHEARSAL. FAMILY CLEARED.

Henry looks at it from the door. He has seen the carbon of the question. He has not seen the answer in Paul's hand until now.

Ward takes a small evidence envelope from his jacket. Inside it is the torn corner from Paul's right hand. He lays the envelope beside the third sheet without opening it. The tear on the sheet runs straight down from the top edge for the length of a hand, and then turns in. The corner in the envelope has the same straight edge and the same short jagged one.

WARD
It went with the grain for the length of a hand. Then it went across. One pair of hands tears across. Two pairs pull along, until one of them lets go.

He does not fit them together. He does not need to.

WARD
Bottom drawer.

The officer opens the bottom drawer of the worktable. Beneath a folder of client proofs is a bundle of typed pages tied in white cotton tape, the knot on top. It has not been untied. Its top sheet is the same heading, typed earlier and without the corrections. It is the draft the loose sheets replaced.

The camera goes off four more times.

Ward counts without touching. Seventeen loose sheets, four of them the overlays and the rest under the proofs. One half sheet. One tied bundle.

WARD
Miss Ellery.

Ruth has not moved from the typewriter table.

RUTH
I picked them up.

WARD
When?

RUTH
After. He had already gone. They were on the floor.

WARD
Last night you said you did not see pages.

RUTH
I said I looked down. Then I looked at what was at my feet.

WARD
You said you did not look in the carton.

RUTH
They were not in the carton.

WARD
Where were they?

RUTH
On the floor. Between the rail and the stair.

WARD
Why take them?

RUTH
My name was on them.

Ward writes that down. He does not ask how she read a name by that light.

Henry, from the door:

HENRY
You told me nothing else was in this room.

Ruth looks at him.

RUTH
I said if something was open on my desk it was not yours.

HENRY
You said you removed nothing but the envelope.

RUTH
I removed nothing. I brought something in.

It is the kind of answer she is good at. She hears that it is, and for the first time since Henry has known her, she looks as if she would take it back.

RUTH
That was—

She stops.

RUTH
I know what that was.

Ward closes the bottom drawer with his shoe.

WARD
Miss Ellery, you will come with me now and make a formal statement about these papers. You are not under arrest for your brother's death. Whether you are charged with anything tonight is not my decision.

RUTH
Whose is it?

WARD
The district attorney's. He will want all of it or none of it.

RUTH
And you?

WARD
I want the rail.

He gives the envelope with the corner back to the officer.

WARD
Calder, you are finished here. Go and tell your client what you saw. Not what it means.

HENRY
I know the difference.

WARD
Then you are the only one in this room who does.

The officers begin bagging the sheets one at a time, in the order they were found. The smiling man on the fourth photograph goes on pointing at the blank rectangle where the price should be.

Ruth takes her coat from behind the inner door. At the threshold she stops beside Henry. She does not touch him.

RUTH
Now you know two things.

HENRY
I knew the second one on Friday. I stood next to it.

RUTH
You did not know what it was.

HENRY
No. I knew it was covered.

She goes down the stairs between the two officers. Ward follows. The frosted glass says RUTH ELLERY, COPYWRITING, in letters larger than Henry's.

Henry waits until the street door closes below. Until it does, some part of him is still hoping the pages had been put there by somebody else. He had not known that part was there.

Then he leaves the office without touching anything and goes to find Nell, to tell her badly.
